      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Apache Parquet\nWhat is a Parquet file, and why\ndoes it store each column\ntogether? [Apache Parquet](https:/\n/parquet.apache.org/) is a file\nformat for the rows and columns of\none table. It stores the values of\neach column next to each other,\nnot row by row. The file also\nholds the column names and types.\n[Apache\nIceberg](../iceberg/iceberg.md)\nuses it as its usual data file\nformat. Snowflake supports Iceberg\ntables that use Parquet files\n([Snowflake documentation](https:/\n/docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/\ntables-iceberg)).\n\nKeeping a column together gives\nthree gains. A reader loads only\nthe columns that a query needs.\nThe writer stores each column in a\ncompact form. A reader skips whole\nblocks of rows, by using the\nminimum and maximum values in the\nfooter. How Much Columnar Saves\nmeasures the gains. The price is\nthat one row is spread over every\ncolumn. An engine, which is a\nprogram that runs queries such as\nSnowflake, also writes each\nParquet file once and replaces it\nto change data. Files Are Written\nOnce explains why.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-acd079a9820e",
      "originalText": "Apache Parquet\nWhat is a Parquet file, and why does it store each column together? [Apache Parquet](https://parquet.apache.org/) is a file format for the rows and columns of one table. It stores the values of each column next to each other, not row by row. The file also holds the column names and types. [Apache Iceberg](../iceberg/iceberg.md) uses it as its usual data file format. Snowflake supports Iceberg tables that use Parquet files ([Snowflake documentation](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/tables-iceberg)).\n\nKeeping a column together gives three gains. A reader loads only the columns that a query needs. The writer stores each column in a compact form. A reader skips whole blocks of rows, by using the minimum and maximum values in the footer. How Much Columnar Saves measures the gains. The price is that one row is spread over every column. An engine, which is a program that runs queries such as Snowflake, also writes each Parquet file once and replaces it to change data. Files Are Written Once explains why.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "apache-parquet"
      }
    },
