      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What Is Parquet?\nA Parquet file stores the data of one table:\nnamed, typed columns and many rows. This map\nfollows one example table with the columns\n`id`, `city`, and `fare`, and six rows: `1,\nPerth, 12.5`; `2, Perth, 8.0`; `3, Sydney,\n20.0`; `4, Sydney, 15.5`; `5, Perth, 9.0`; and\n`6, Perth, 11.0`. A short script writes the\ntable to the file `trips.parquet`, which takes\n1,595 bytes. The file is stored beside this\nmap, and About the Numbers shows the script.\nThe file also stores the type of each column:\n`id` is an integer (`int64`), `city` is text\n(`string`), and `fare` is a decimal number\n(`double`). The file stores each value as the\nbytes of its number or text. With plain\nencoding, which writes each value as it is,\nthe number 12.5 is the 8 bytes `00 00 00 00 00\n00 29 40`. The writer of `trips.parquet` also\ncompresses the bytes with SNAPPY, a\ncompression method, so the file holds their\ncompressed form (Compress the Bytes explains\nit). A CSV file stores the same number as the\nfour characters `12.5`, which are the bytes\n`31 32 2e 35`. A tool that reads Parquet\ndecodes the bytes into rows and columns.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-fb8d4ceb6ff8",
      "originalText": "What Is Parquet?\nA Parquet file stores the data of one table: named, typed columns and many rows. This map follows one example table with the columns `id`, `city`, and `fare`, and six rows: `1, Perth, 12.5`; `2, Perth, 8.0`; `3, Sydney, 20.0`; `4, Sydney, 15.5`; `5, Perth, 9.0`; and `6, Perth, 11.0`. A short script writes the table to the file `trips.parquet`, which takes 1,595 bytes. The file is stored beside this map, and About the Numbers shows the script. The file also stores the type of each column: `id` is an integer (`int64`), `city` is text (`string`), and `fare` is a decimal number (`double`). The file stores each value as the bytes of its number or text. With plain encoding, which writes each value as it is, the number 12.5 is the 8 bytes `00 00 00 00 00 00 29 40`. The writer of `trips.parquet` also compresses the bytes with SNAPPY, a compression method, so the file holds their compressed form (Compress the Bytes explains it). A CSV file stores the same number as the four characters `12.5`, which are the bytes `31 32 2e 35`. A tool that reads Parquet decodes the bytes into rows and columns.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "what-is-parquet"
      }
    },
