    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 9295207550,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-e12dc140eaea",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1002.0,
      "y": 392.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 157.5,
      "seed": 872782,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Row Layout\nA row layout stores one record after another.\nA CSV (comma-separated values) file is the\nusual example. The example table becomes six\ntext lines: `1,Perth,12.5`, then\n`2,Perth,8.0`, then `3,Sydney,20.0`, and so\non. The three values of one record sit next to\neach other. To read only `fare`, a reader must\nscan every line and cut out the third value.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-e12dc140eaea",
      "originalText": "Row Layout\nA row layout stores one record after another. A CSV (comma-separated values) file is the usual example. The example table becomes six text lines: `1,Perth,12.5`, then `2,Perth,8.0`, then `3,Sydney,20.0`, and so on. The three values of one record sit next to each other. To read only `fare`, a reader must scan every line and cut out the third value.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "row-layout"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3446031743,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-47455e3cb314",
