    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 6153598580,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-b07779aeda7b",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1889.6000000000001,
      "y": 2665.75,
      "strokeColor": "#1F2937",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 397.8,
      "height": 357.5,
      "seed": 577856,
      "groupIds": [
        "rhdjcntn"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Compress the Bytes\nThe writer then compresses each page with a codec (\n[specification](https://github.com/apache/parquet-f\normat/blob/ec90dd4/Compression.md#L26-L29)), and\nthe footer records the codec of each chunk. Every\nchunk in the example file uses SNAPPY, which is the\ndefault codec in pyarrow ([`write_table`](https://a\nrrow.apache.org/docs/python/generated/pyarrow.parqu\net.write_table.html)). SNAPPY helps when bytes\nrepeat ([format](https://github.com/google/snappy/b\nlob/main/format_description.txt#L31-L42)). GZIP\n([RFC\n1951](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1951)) and\nZSTD ([RFC\n8878](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8878)) also\ngive common bytes shorter bit codes. On the plain\n`city` column, SNAPPY cuts 3,299,981 bytes to\n593,977. After dictionary encoding, little is left\nto remove: 75,933 bytes become 75,957. The random\ntext of the `note` column barely shrinks with\nSNAPPY. Its 13,340,241 encoded bytes become\n12,872,675 compressed bytes, which is 3.5% less.",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 16.25,
      "containerId": "node-b07779aeda7b",
      "originalText": "Compress the Bytes\nThe writer then compresses each page with a codec ([specification](https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/ec90dd4/Compression.md#L26-L29)), and the footer records the codec of each chunk. Every chunk in the example file uses SNAPPY, which is the default codec in pyarrow ([`write_table`](https://arrow.apache.org/docs/python/generated/pyarrow.parquet.write_table.html)). SNAPPY helps when bytes repeat ([format](https://github.com/google/snappy/blob/main/format_description.txt#L31-L42)). GZIP ([RFC 1951](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1951)) and ZSTD ([RFC 8878](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8878)) also give common bytes shorter bit codes. On the plain `city` column, SNAPPY cuts 3,299,981 bytes to 593,977. After dictionary encoding, little is left to remove: 75,933 bytes become 75,957. The random text of the `note` column barely shrinks with SNAPPY. Its 13,340,241 encoded bytes become 12,872,675 compressed bytes, which is 3.5% less.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "compress-bytes"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 4481128311,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-be3163a8d496",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1289.2,
      "y": 2925.75,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "#F8FAFC",
      "width": 418.8,
      "height": 220.0,
      "seed": 359125,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3,
        "value": 8
      },
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
