        "node_id": "row-group-skipping"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7124292512,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-be3163a8d496",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1301.2,
      "y": 2925.75,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 735.0,
      "seed": 116198,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Row Group Skipping\nThe footer stores the minimum and maximum\nvalue of each column chunk ([`Statistics`](htt\nps://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/ec9\n0dd4/src/main/thrift/parquet.thrift#L267-L311)\n). A reader that filters on a column compares\nthe filter with these values. It skips every\nrow group that cannot match. In the example\nfile, `id` runs from 1 to 3 in row group 0 and\nfrom 4 to 6 in row group 1. The filter `id >\n3` keeps only row group 1.\n\nSkipping works only when similar values sit in\nthe same row group. In the example, `fare`\nruns from 8.0 to 20.0 in row group 0 and from\n9.0 to 15.5 in row group 1. The filter `fare >\n12` therefore keeps both. In the test file,\n`id` is sorted, and `id >= 200000` keeps one\nof three row groups. Its `fare` values are\nrandom, so every row group runs from 5.0 to\n60.0. The filter `fare > 55` keeps all three.\nThese results come from the pyarrow dataset\nAPI in November 2026.\n\nSnowflake tables use the same idea. Snowflake\nkeeps the range of values of each column for\neach micro-partition. A query skips the\nmicro-partitions that cannot match ([micro-par\ntitions](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-gu\nide/tables-clustering-micropartitions)).\nApache Iceberg adds a level above the footer.\nEach manifest records the minimum and maximum\nvalue of each column for every data file. A\nreader therefore skips whole files first. The\n[Iceberg map](../iceberg/iceberg.md) describes\nmanifests. Inside each file that remains,\nIceberg's Parquet reader compares the footer\nminimum and maximum values with the filter. It\nskips row groups that cannot match ([`ReadConf\n`](https://github.com/apache/iceberg/blob/4616\nacf/parquet/src/main/java/org/apache/iceberg/p\narquet/ReadConf.java#L94-L114)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-be3163a8d496",
      "originalText": "Row Group Skipping\nThe footer stores the minimum and maximum value of each column chunk ([`Statistics`](https://github.com/apache/parquet-format/blob/ec90dd4/src/main/thrift/parquet.thrift#L267-L311)). A reader that filters on a column compares the filter with these values. It skips every row group that cannot match. In the example file, `id` runs from 1 to 3 in row group 0 and from 4 to 6 in row group 1. The filter `id > 3` keeps only row group 1.\n\nSkipping works only when similar values sit in the same row group. In the example, `fare` runs from 8.0 to 20.0 in row group 0 and from 9.0 to 15.5 in row group 1. The filter `fare > 12` therefore keeps both. In the test file, `id` is sorted, and `id >= 200000` keeps one of three row groups. Its `fare` values are random, so every row group runs from 5.0 to 60.0. The filter `fare > 55` keeps all three. These results come from the pyarrow dataset API in November 2026.\n\nSnowflake tables use the same idea. Snowflake keeps the range of values of each column for each micro-partition. A query skips the micro-partitions that cannot match ([micro-partitions](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/tables-clustering-micropartitions)). Apache Iceberg adds a level above the footer. Each manifest records the minimum and maximum value of each column for every data file. A reader therefore skips whole files first. The [Iceberg map](../iceberg/iceberg.md) describes manifests. Inside each file that remains, Iceberg's Parquet reader compares the footer minimum and maximum values with the filter. It skips row groups that cannot match ([`ReadConf`](https://github.com/apache/iceberg/blob/4616acf/parquet/src/main/java/org/apache/iceberg/parquet/ReadConf.java#L94-L114)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "row-group-skipping"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 6949420276,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-773d311b5dc5",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1289.2,
      "y": 3185.75,
      "strokeColor": "#92400E",
      "backgroundColor": "#FEF3C7",
      "width": 410.40000000000003,
      "height": 164.0,
      "seed": 730893,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": {
        "type": 3,
        "value": 8
      },
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "type": "text",
