    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2697302301,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-7d0da0082d57",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1074.4,
      "y": 3389.75,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 507.5,
      "seed": 186592,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Open a Parquet File\nA text editor shows a Parquet file as\nunreadable characters. Tools that read Parquet\ndecode the bytes and show rows and columns:\n\n- Snowflake: a stage is a named place for data\nfiles, inside Snowflake or in cloud storage.\nAfter you put the file in a stage, Snowflake\nreads each Parquet row into one column, `$1`.\nThe query `SELECT $1:id, $1:city::varchar,\n$1:fare FROM @my_stage/trips.parquet;` returns\nthe example columns ([Parquet tutorial](https:\n//docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/tutorials/s\ncript-data-load-transform-parquet)). The\nfunction `INFER_SCHEMA` lists the column names\nand types that the file stores ([`INFER_SCHEMA\n`](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference\n/functions/infer_schema)).\n- pyarrow:\n`pyarrow.parquet.read_table('trips.parquet')`\nreturns a table ([`read_table`](https://arrow.\napache.org/docs/python/generated/pyarrow.parqu\net.read_table.html)). Its `to_pydict()` method\nreturns the dictionary of columns.\n- pandas, the Python data-analysis library:\n`pandas.read_parquet('trips.parquet')` uses\npyarrow, or fastparquet if pyarrow is missing \n([`read_parquet`](https://pandas.pydata.org/do\ncs/reference/api/pandas.read_parquet.html)).",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-7d0da0082d57",
      "originalText": "Open a Parquet File\nA text editor shows a Parquet file as unreadable characters. Tools that read Parquet decode the bytes and show rows and columns:\n\n- Snowflake: a stage is a named place for data files, inside Snowflake or in cloud storage. After you put the file in a stage, Snowflake reads each Parquet row into one column, `$1`. The query `SELECT $1:id, $1:city::varchar, $1:fare FROM @my_stage/trips.parquet;` returns the example columns ([Parquet tutorial](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/tutorials/script-data-load-transform-parquet)). The function `INFER_SCHEMA` lists the column names and types that the file stores ([`INFER_SCHEMA`](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/functions/infer_schema)).\n- pyarrow: `pyarrow.parquet.read_table('trips.parquet')` returns a table ([`read_table`](https://arrow.apache.org/docs/python/generated/pyarrow.parquet.read_table.html)). Its `to_pydict()` method returns the dictionary of columns.\n- pandas, the Python data-analysis library: `pandas.read_parquet('trips.parquet')` uses pyarrow, or fastparquet if pyarrow is missing ([`read_parquet`](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.read_parquet.html)).",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "open-a-parquet-file"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
