    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2018206677,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-3bac4ac70436",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1074.4,
      "y": 3888.75,
      "strokeColor": "#92400E",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 525.0,
      "seed": 499432,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Tiny Files Waste Space\nEvery Parquet file carries its own footer. The\nexample file spends 981 of its 1,595 bytes on\nthe footer, which is more than the 602 bytes\nof column data. The test file spends 1,893 of\nits 15,353,361 bytes, which is 0.01%. A reader\nalso makes one extra read for each file, to\nget the footer. Many tiny files therefore\nwaste storage and time.\n\nSnowflake and Iceberg give sizes to aim for.\nSnowflake recommends data files of roughly 100\nto 250 MB compressed for loading ([loading gui\nde](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/d\nata-load-considerations-prepare)). For a\nSnowflake-managed Iceberg table, the setting\n`TARGET_FILE_SIZE` sets a target Parquet file\nsize. The choices are `AUTO`, 16 MB, 32 MB, 64\nMB, and 128 MB ([`CREATE ICEBERG TABLE`](https\n://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/cre\nate-iceberg-table-snowflake)). A streaming\nwriter can leave small files when it commits\noften. The Iceberg sink connector in the\n[Kafka \u2192 Iceberg \u2192 Snowflake map](../integrati\non/kafka-iceberg-snowflake/kafka-iceberg-snowf\nlake.md) commits on a timer. Each of its tasks\nwrites its own Parquet files. Compaction in\nIceberg rewrites many small files into fewer\nlarge ones. The [Iceberg\nmap](../iceberg/iceberg.md) describes it.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-3bac4ac70436",
      "originalText": "Tiny Files Waste Space\nEvery Parquet file carries its own footer. The example file spends 981 of its 1,595 bytes on the footer, which is more than the 602 bytes of column data. The test file spends 1,893 of its 15,353,361 bytes, which is 0.01%. A reader also makes one extra read for each file, to get the footer. Many tiny files therefore waste storage and time.\n\nSnowflake and Iceberg give sizes to aim for. Snowflake recommends data files of roughly 100 to 250 MB compressed for loading ([loading guide](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/data-load-considerations-prepare)). For a Snowflake-managed Iceberg table, the setting `TARGET_FILE_SIZE` sets a target Parquet file size. The choices are `AUTO`, 16 MB, 32 MB, 64 MB, and 128 MB ([`CREATE ICEBERG TABLE`](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/create-iceberg-table-snowflake)). A streaming writer can leave small files when it commits often. The Iceberg sink connector in the [Kafka \u2192 Iceberg \u2192 Snowflake map](../integration/kafka-iceberg-snowflake/kafka-iceberg-snowflake.md) commits on a timer. Each of its tasks writes its own Parquet files. Compaction in Iceberg rewrites many small files into fewer large ones. The [Iceberg map](../iceberg/iceberg.md) describes it.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "tiny-files-waste-space"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 8623922557,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-3920b1c5238c",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
