    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 5956598253,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-3920b1c5238c",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 720.0,
      "y": 4148.75,
      "strokeColor": "#57534E",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 397.8,
      "height": 1397.5,
      "seed": 801237,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "About the Numbers\nThe numbers in this map come from files that\nscripts generated in November 2026. The example\nfile `trips.parquet` is stored beside this map, in\nthe folder `mind-maps/de/parquet/` of the mind-map\nrepository. The script below writes it again and\nalso creates the test file `trips_big.parquet`. Run\nthe script in an empty folder, because it writes\nboth files to the current folder. It uses [pyarrow]\n(https://arrow.apache.org/docs/python/generated/pya\nrrow.parquet.write_table.html) 18.1.0:\n\n```python\nimport random\n\nimport pyarrow as pa\nimport pyarrow.parquet as pq\n\nexample = pa.table(\n{\n\"id\": pa.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], pa.int64()),\n\"city\": [\"Perth\", \"Perth\", \"Sydney\", \"Sydney\",\n\"Perth\", \"Perth\"],\n\"fare\": [12.5, 8.0, 20.0, 15.5, 9.0, 11.0],\n}\n)\npq.write_table(example, \"trips.parquet\",\nrow_group_size=3)\n\nrng = random.Random(42)\nn = 300_000\ncities = [\"Perth\", \"Sydney\", \"Melbourne\",\n\"Brisbane\"]\nletters = \"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789\"\ntest = pa.table(\n{\n\"id\": pa.array(range(n), pa.int64()),\n\"city\": [rng.choice(cities) for _ in range(n)],\n\"fare\": [round(rng.uniform(5, 60), 2) for _ in\nrange(n)],\n\"note\": [\"\".join(rng.choices(letters, k=40)) for _\nin range(n)],\n}\n)\npq.write_table(test, \"trips_big.parquet\",\nrow_group_size=100_000)\n```\n\nThe example file `trips.parquet` holds the six rows\nof the example table in two row groups of three\nrows. It takes 1,595 bytes. The test file\n`trips_big.parquet` holds 300,000 rows in three row\ngroups of 100,000 rows. It takes 15,353,361 bytes.\nOther writers make files of other sizes.\n\nThe CSV file is the test table written with\n`pyarrow.csv.write_csv`, which puts text in quotes.\nIt takes 19,625,110 bytes. The SQLite file holds\nthe same rows in the table `t (id INTEGER PRIMARY\nKEY, city TEXT, fare REAL, note TEXT)`. It takes\n20,017,152 bytes. Each query ran in Python on one\nthread, with pyarrow and the `sqlite3` module. The\nParquet queries used the footer statistics to\nchoose row groups.\n\nThe width test wrote 100,000 rows of decimal\nnumbers with the expression `round(rng.uniform(0,\n100), 2)` from `random.Random(7)`. The columns are\n`c0`, `c1`, and so on, and the row groups have\n50,000 rows.\n\nThe compression sizes compare columns of the test\ntable. Each CSV column is written with\n`pyarrow.csv.write_csv` and compressed with ZSTD\nthrough `pyarrow.compress`. Each Parquet column\ncomes from `pyarrow.parquet.write_table` with\n`compression=\"zstd\"`. The `city` and `fare` columns\nuse dictionary encoding. The `id` column uses\n`column_encoding={\"id\": \"DELTA_BINARY_PACKED\"}`,\nwith `use_dictionary` listing the other columns.\nThe whole-table sizes compare the whole CSV file\nwith the whole Parquet file under the same codec.\nThe one-column read sums `fare` from a\nZSTD-compressed CSV file and from the Parquet file,\nwith the method of the queries above. The estimates\nin the text round these results.",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 16.25,
      "containerId": "node-3920b1c5238c",
      "originalText": "About the Numbers\nThe numbers in this map come from files that scripts generated in November 2026. The example file `trips.parquet` is stored beside this map, in the folder `mind-maps/de/parquet/` of the mind-map repository. The script below writes it again and also creates the test file `trips_big.parquet`. Run the script in an empty folder, because it writes both files to the current folder. It uses [pyarrow](https://arrow.apache.org/docs/python/generated/pyarrow.parquet.write_table.html) 18.1.0:\n\n```python\nimport random\n\nimport pyarrow as pa\nimport pyarrow.parquet as pq\n\nexample = pa.table(\n    {\n        \"id\": pa.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], pa.int64()),\n        \"city\": [\"Perth\", \"Perth\", \"Sydney\", \"Sydney\", \"Perth\", \"Perth\"],\n        \"fare\": [12.5, 8.0, 20.0, 15.5, 9.0, 11.0],\n    }\n)\npq.write_table(example, \"trips.parquet\", row_group_size=3)\n\nrng = random.Random(42)\nn = 300_000\ncities = [\"Perth\", \"Sydney\", \"Melbourne\", \"Brisbane\"]\nletters = \"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789\"\ntest = pa.table(\n    {\n        \"id\": pa.array(range(n), pa.int64()),\n        \"city\": [rng.choice(cities) for _ in range(n)],\n        \"fare\": [round(rng.uniform(5, 60), 2) for _ in range(n)],\n        \"note\": [\"\".join(rng.choices(letters, k=40)) for _ in range(n)],\n    }\n)\npq.write_table(test, \"trips_big.parquet\", row_group_size=100_000)\n```\n\nThe example file `trips.parquet` holds the six rows of the example table in two row groups of three rows. It takes 1,595 bytes. The test file `trips_big.parquet` holds 300,000 rows in three row groups of 100,000 rows. It takes 15,353,361 bytes. Other writers make files of other sizes.\n\nThe CSV file is the test table written with `pyarrow.csv.write_csv`, which puts text in quotes. It takes 19,625,110 bytes. The SQLite file holds the same rows in the table `t (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, city TEXT, fare REAL, note TEXT)`. It takes 20,017,152 bytes. Each query ran in Python on one thread, with pyarrow and the `sqlite3` module. The Parquet queries used the footer statistics to choose row groups.\n\nThe width test wrote 100,000 rows of decimal numbers with the expression `round(rng.uniform(0, 100), 2)` from `random.Random(7)`. The columns are `c0`, `c1`, and so on, and the row groups have 50,000 rows.\n\nThe compression sizes compare columns of the test table. Each CSV column is written with `pyarrow.csv.write_csv` and compressed with ZSTD through `pyarrow.compress`. Each Parquet column comes from `pyarrow.parquet.write_table` with `compression=\"zstd\"`. The `city` and `fare` columns use dictionary encoding. The `id` column uses `column_encoding={\"id\": \"DELTA_BINARY_PACKED\"}`, with `use_dictionary` listing the other columns. The whole-table sizes compare the whole CSV file with the whole Parquet file under the same codec. The one-column read sums `fare` from a ZSTD-compressed CSV file and from the Parquet file, with the method of the queries above. The estimates in the text round these results.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "about-the-numbers"
      }
    },
